36. ENGINEERING RULES
========================================================

Follow these rules throughout implementation:

1. Never invent requirements silently.
2. If an assumption is necessary, document it.
3. Do not remove existing working functionality without explaining why.
4. Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
5. Prefer server components by default.
6. Do not fetch your own internal API unnecessarily from server components.
7. Validate external/user input.
8. Authorize every protected mutation.
9. Apply organization scope to database queries.
10. Avoid N+1 queries.
11. Use database transactions for multi-step operations that must remain consistent.
12. Do not hard-code business statuses across many files.
13. Keep enums/constants centralized.
14. Do not mix database models with UI models unnecessarily.
15. Never expose secrets to NEXT_PUBLIC environment variables.
16. Never put sensitive data into logs.
17. Do not use `any` merely to silence TypeScript.
18. Keep TypeScript strict.
19. Do not ignore lint/type errors.
20. Never declare a phase complete if the application does not build.

